## The Cartesian coordinate system in space

In space, the Cartesian coordinate system is three mutually perpendicular number axes, the $x$-, $y$- and $z$-axis, that intersect at the origin. Every point in space is represented by exactly one ordered triple $(x, y, z)$.

<CoordinateSystems />

## Distance between two points in space

The distance between two points $A(x_{1}, y_{1}, z_{1})$ and $B(x_{2}, y_{2}, z_{2})$ in space is also the hypotenuse of a right triangle, $ACB$. We just need one more right triangle, $ADC$, lying flat underneath it, to get the length of the leg $AC$ first. Take your time with the demo below, and drag it sideways to turn the view.

$|AD| = |x_{2} - x_{1}|$,
$|DC| = |z_{2} - z_{1}|$,
$|CB| = |y_{2} - y_{1}|$

From triangle $ADC$:

$$
|AC|^2 = (x_{2} - x_{1})^2 + (z_{2} - z_{1})^2
$$

and from triangle $ACB$:

$$
|AB| = \sqrt{|AC|^2 + (y_{2} - y_{1})^2} = \sqrt{(x_{2} - x_{1})^2 + (y_{2} - y_{1})^2 + (z_{2} - z_{1})^2}
$$

<DistanceDemo3D />

<CodeGroup>

```ts
type Point3 = { x: number; y: number; z: number };

function distance3(a: Point3, b: Point3): number {
	return Math.sqrt((b.x - a.x) ** 2 + (b.y - a.y) ** 2 + (b.z - a.z) ** 2);
}
```

```js
function distance3(a, b) {
	return Math.sqrt((b.x - a.x) ** 2 + (b.y - a.y) ** 2 + (b.z - a.z) ** 2);
}
```

```py
@dataclass
class Point3:
    x: float
    y: float
    z: float


def distance3(a: Point3, b: Point3) -> float:
    return math.sqrt((b.x - a.x) ** 2 + (b.y - a.y) ** 2 + (b.z - a.z) ** 2)
```

</CodeGroup>

Each extra dimension just adds one more squared difference under the root.
